+# Members get no receipts
+
+For the volunteer on call when members report that no receipt emails arrive. Brings the worker back so the queued
+receipts go out; while it is down, every captured pantry order waits for its receipt. Written after it happened twice:
+the first time nobody on call knew what to do and members waited until noon, and on September 14 Tomás found the worker
+stopped since a deploy at 23:02, because the deploy script restarts only the web app. Owner: Tomás.
+
+## Before you begin
+
+- A shell on the server, with `sudo` for `systemctl` and `journalctl`.
+- `psql` access to the `larder` database.
+
+## Steps
+
+1. Check the [worker](../architecture.md#parts), which sends receipts: `systemctl status larder-worker`.
+
+   Expected: `inactive (dead)`, often since the time of the last deploy. If it is `active (running)`, the worker is
+   not the cause; go to [If it fails](#if-it-fails).
+2. Count the jobs waiting before restarting it:
+   `psql larder -c "select status, count(*) from procrastinate_jobs group by status"`.
+
+   Expected: `todo` in the tens or hundreds, `doing` at 0. Note the numbers.
+3. Start the worker: `sudo systemctl start larder-worker`.
+
+   Expected: `systemctl status larder-worker` shows `active (running)`.
+4. Run the query of step 2 again each minute or two.
+
+   Expected: `todo` goes down, about 40 a minute on September 14, and reaches 0. A few `failed` jobs are receipts
+   that bounced because the member has no valid email address; they need no action.
+
+## If it fails
+
+If the worker stops again right after starting, read its last lines, `journalctl -u larder-worker -n 50`, and send
+them to Tomás in #larder-ops. If the worker is running but `todo` does not go down, or members still get no receipts
+once it reaches 0, ask Tomás in #larder-ops too.
